2026-09-21 | Stage 6 media URLs signed at serialization time -- THE FIX | The clips, thumbnails and subtitles all existed and were all unreachable from a browser | Sign download_url, thumbnail_url and srt_url in _job_to_response -- the single funnel for GET /api/jobs and GET /api/jobs/{id} -- leaving the persisted values in outputs/jobs.json unsigned, so no stored record carries an expiry that outlives it. Applied the same decorator to the SSE completed frame: inert today because JobDetail.jsx discards those clips and re-fetches, but a serialization site emitting UNSIGNED media URLs is what the next consumer adopts by accident. A URL that already has a query string is left alone, so signing cannot be applied twice. VERIFIED END TO END over HTTP against the human's real job 2773bd83c7b6: all 7 clips' mp4 + jpg + srt fetch with NO HEADERS AT ALL and answer 206 to a Range request, ?download=1 gives attachment with the right filename, two consecutive reads return byte-identical URLs, the token appears nowhere in the response, the same URL stripped of its signature is still 401 application/json (the exact response that was being saved as .json), that signature pasted onto /api/jobs and /api/shutdown is 401, and jobs.json was never written | web/api/routes/jobs.py, tests/test_clip_serving.py (+10)
2026-09-21 | Stage 7 the dashboard clip card | With the backend fixed the card still drew a black rectangle per clip, offered no way to get the .srt out, and would stall silently once a signature aged out | poster={clip.thumbnail_url} so seven thumbnails appear before a byte of video is fetched; download={clip.filename} on the anchor so the saved name is right even where Content-Disposition is ignored on a same-origin link; a .srt button when clip.srt_url exists; and onError -> one re-fetch of the job to mint fresh signatures, guarded by a flag so a genuinely broken file cannot become a reload loop. The download flag is appended through a single asDownload() helper that uses & when the URL already has a query string -- it always does -- because the naive '?download=1' makes sig part of the previous parameter's value and produces a 401 indistinguishable from a signing bug. Four source-read tests pin all of that, since every one of those failures is silent | web/dashboard/src/pages/JobDetail.jsx, tests/test_clip_serving.py (+4)
